1
00:00:03,060 --> 00:00:05,860
Hello, everyone, and welcome back to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:06,580
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:07,180 --> 00:00:10,720
And my name is Catherine, and we've got a great lesson for you today.

4
00:00:10,900 --> 00:00:11,880
What level is this, Marco?

5
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:17,800
This is an elementary lesson, and we're going to be touching one of these medical topics.

6
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:19,960
Today we're going to talk about chickenpox.

7
00:00:20,120 --> 00:00:23,440
Oh, gosh, I hated the chickenpox.

8
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:27,610
They're horrible, and they itch, and you can't do anything about it, and your parents say, don't scratch.

9
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:29,680
Right.

10
00:00:29,680 --> 00:00:39,010
So chickenpox is an illness that you get usually as a child, and what happens is you start getting these little red dots all over your body, like little mosquito bites.

11
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:49,450
Exactly, and they come all over your body, arms, legs, face, and you can only get this disease once, unless maybe you didn't really get it too seriously.

12
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:51,840
So most people get this when they're children.

13
00:00:51,840 --> 00:01:05,490
Exactly, and what makes you very uncomfortable when you get chickenpox, as you said, it's these little red dots, they itch, just like mosquito bites, but you can't scratch because if you do, then your skin gets like marked, right?

14
00:01:05,720 --> 00:01:06,720
You have like stains.

15
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:07,720
Forever.

16
00:01:07,720 --> 00:01:08,920
Forever, like scars.

17
00:01:08,920 --> 00:01:13,160
So it's best not to scratch, and that's what we're going to talk about today.

18
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:15,740
Somebody has the chickenpox.

19
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:19,440
So let's listen to our dialogue for the first time, and then we'll be right back.

20
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:28,520
What's wrong with you?

21
00:01:28,760 --> 00:01:30,300
Why are you scratching so much?

22
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:32,180
I feel itchy.

23
00:01:32,640 --> 00:01:33,900
I can't stand it anymore.

24
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:36,180
I think I may be coming down with something.

25
00:01:36,400 --> 00:01:37,780
I feel lightheaded and weak.

26
00:01:38,580 --> 00:01:39,420
Let me have a look.

27
00:01:40,500 --> 00:01:41,020
Whoa!

28
00:01:41,340 --> 00:01:42,200
Get away from me!

29
00:01:42,480 --> 00:01:43,200
What's wrong?

30
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:45,560
I think you have chicken pox.

31
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:47,000
You're contagious.

32
00:01:47,780 --> 00:01:48,120
Get away.

33
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:49,120
Don't breathe on me.

34
00:01:49,120 --> 00:01:51,540
Maybe it's just a rash or an allergy.

35
00:01:51,940 --> 00:01:53,820
We can't be sure until I see a doctor.

36
00:01:54,380 --> 00:01:56,500
Well, in the meantime, you're a biohazard.

37
00:01:57,020 --> 00:02:01,520
I didn't get it when I was a kid, and I've heard that you can even die if you get it as an adult.

38
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:02,700
Are you serious?

39
00:02:02,980 --> 00:02:04,760
You always blow things out of proportion.

40
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:12,660
In any case, I think I'll go take an oatmeal bath. Eww.

41
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:17,300
Alright, we're back, so I think this kid does have the chicken pox.

42
00:02:17,820 --> 00:02:21,820
Definitely, and well, he's got a funny solution for it.

43
00:02:21,980 --> 00:02:30,660
But before we get there, we've got a lot of great medical words and just basic body words for you in today's Language Takeaway.

44
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:31,060
Language Takeaway.

45
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:35,840
Alright, so let's start with our first word.

46
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:37,740
He mentioned that he felt itchy.

47
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:38,760
I feel itchy.

48
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:39,320
Itchy.

49
00:02:40,080 --> 00:02:44,600
Okay, this is often confused even among children who are native speakers of English.

50
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:47,120
There are two words, to itch and to scratch.

51
00:02:47,700 --> 00:02:52,420
But this person is itchy, so it means that your skin, you want to scratch it.

52
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:54,120
It's maybe uncomfortable.

53
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:57,700
Like a mosquito bite is itchy.

54
00:02:57,860 --> 00:03:00,360
Okay, so itchy would be the adjective, right?

55
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:03,740
To the noun, an itch.

56
00:03:03,740 --> 00:03:06,210
So if you have an itch, you feel itchy, right?

57
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:07,010
Exactly.

58
00:03:07,740 --> 00:03:10,220
So, and that's when you scratch.

59
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:15,860
Alright so he feels itchy and he also felt a little bit lightheaded.

60
00:03:16,250 --> 00:03:18,990
Okay, so you know the word head, you know the word light.

61
00:03:19,470 --> 00:03:21,890
So here we have a person who feels lightheaded.

62
00:03:22,020 --> 00:03:30,020
It means that you kind of feel a little bit strange and your head is a little bit, well, what's a good word to describe it?

63
00:03:30,020 --> 00:03:31,020
Like dizzy.

64
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:32,010
Yeah, almost dizzy or faint.

65
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:33,700
Right.

66
00:03:33,700 --> 00:03:35,330
So, you usually feel a little bit weak.

67
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:37,310
You don't really feel very well.

68
00:03:37,530 --> 00:03:38,970
Your head is lightheaded.

69
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:41,450
The complete opposite of having a headache, right?

70
00:03:41,750 --> 00:03:41,950
Exactly.

71
00:03:42,510 --> 00:03:48,170
Okay, and well then the girl got scared and she said, get away, you are contagious.

72
00:03:48,990 --> 00:03:50,790
Oh gosh, now this is a dangerous one.

73
00:03:51,460 --> 00:03:56,120
Someone who is contagious can pass their illness to someone else.

74
00:03:56,120 --> 00:04:07,190
So for example, the flu is very contagious and if I have the flu, Marco doesn't want me to, you know, come over and touch all of his stuff and sneeze and cough into his food.

75
00:04:07,190 --> 00:04:08,430
Right, exactly.

76
00:04:08,860 --> 00:04:09,860
So that's contagious.

77
00:04:09,860 --> 00:04:15,540
It's an adjective and that means that you easily pass your illness to another person.

78
00:04:15,610 --> 00:04:15,850
Exactly.

79
00:04:16,540 --> 00:04:23,260
Alright, so why don't we take a look at a couple of different examples using this word contagious.

80
00:04:23,260 --> 00:04:23,550
Example one.

81
00:04:25,860 --> 00:04:26,640
Don't get near me.

82
00:04:26,940 --> 00:04:28,300
Chicken pox are very contagious.

83
00:04:30,140 --> 00:04:30,960
Example two.

84
00:04:32,650 --> 00:04:35,650
The sickness that is going around the office is very contagious.

85
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:39,380
Example three.

86
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:43,820
H1N1 flu is very contagious.

87
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:50,700
Alright, so you want to avoid contagious people but you might also want to avoid people who have this next word.

88
00:04:50,700 --> 00:04:51,840
It's called a rash.

89
00:04:52,340 --> 00:04:53,460
A rash, exactly.

90
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:56,440
So why do you want to avoid people who have a rash?

91
00:04:57,180 --> 00:04:58,680
They might have a contagious rash.

92
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:02,620
Who knows but you can get a rash for a number of different reasons.

93
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:11,580
A rash is just basically a part of your skin that becomes irritated or uncomfortable and sometimes it turns red or pink.

94
00:05:11,580 --> 00:05:12,580
Exactly.

95
00:05:12,580 --> 00:05:14,830
So, as you said, it happens for different reasons.

96
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:22,660
Sometimes you maybe have bad seafood in your skin and you start to have rashes all over your body.

97
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:28,350
Other times you can touch some plants that are dangerous like poison ivy or poison oak.

98
00:05:29,340 --> 00:05:31,300
And then you get a big rash.

99
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:32,990
And our last word for today is a little bit dramatic.

100
00:05:34,060 --> 00:05:35,810
She called him a biohazard.

101
00:05:36,720 --> 00:05:37,100
Biohazard.

102
00:05:37,100 --> 00:05:41,020
Well, let's take a look at these words individually.

103
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:43,240
The first word bio comes from the word biology.

104
00:05:43,900 --> 00:05:45,900
So that's something that is alive.

105
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:48,180
An animal, a plant or a person.

106
00:05:48,180 --> 00:05:50,900
And hazard is something that is dangerous.

107
00:05:50,900 --> 00:05:55,300
So a biohazard is a, well, something that's alive that's dangerous.

108
00:05:56,060 --> 00:05:56,440
Right.

109
00:05:57,180 --> 00:06:00,220
Something that's dangerous towards a living person, right, or organism.

110
00:06:00,220 --> 00:06:04,200
For example, you don't want to be near an area that has a lot of radiation.

111
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:05,460
It's a biohazard.

112
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:06,380
Exactly.

113
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:11,300
Or some of these diseases like Ebola virus.

114
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:13,280
These are very dangerous towards living things.

115
00:06:13,580 --> 00:06:16,420
And so biohazard is the name for it.

116
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:17,420
Exactly.

117
00:06:17,420 --> 00:06:23,140
And usually there's a symbol, a very clear symbol that kind of looks like a triangle and it's red.

118
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:25,080
And even in movies it's appeared, right?

119
00:06:25,180 --> 00:06:26,180
I think in that movie.

120
00:06:26,180 --> 00:06:27,180
Yellow.

121
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:27,880
It's red or yellow.

122
00:06:28,180 --> 00:06:28,640
Yeah, sometimes.

123
00:06:29,180 --> 00:06:30,180
All right.

124
00:06:30,280 --> 00:06:31,540
So biohazard.

125
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:33,900
I think we've taken a look at a lot of words.

126
00:06:33,900 --> 00:06:36,480
So why don't we listen to this dialogue again?

127
00:06:36,860 --> 00:06:38,120
Let's slow it down a little bit.

128
00:06:38,660 --> 00:06:40,080
See if we can understand a little bit more.

129
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:46,920
What's wrong with you?

130
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:49,600
Why are you scratching so much?

131
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:52,160
I feel itchy.

132
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:54,600
I can't stand it anymore.

133
00:06:55,220 --> 00:06:57,900
I think I may be coming down with something.

134
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:00,060
I feel lightheaded and weak.

135
00:07:00,840 --> 00:07:02,300
Let me have a look.

136
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:03,780
Whoa.

137
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:05,620
Get away from me.

138
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:06,980
What's wrong?

139
00:07:08,200 --> 00:07:10,480
I think you have the chicken pox.

140
00:07:11,200 --> 00:07:11,820
You're contagious.

141
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:13,340
Get away.

142
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:15,100
Don't breathe on me.

143
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:18,000
Maybe it's just a rash or an allergy.

144
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:20,600
We can't be sure until I see a doctor.

145
00:07:21,060 --> 00:07:24,920
Well, in the meantime, you are a biohazard.

146
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:31,360
I didn't get it when I was a kid and I've heard that you can even die if you get it as an adult.

147
00:07:31,930 --> 00:07:32,710
Are you serious?

148
00:07:33,670 --> 00:07:35,490
You always blow things out of proportion.

149
00:07:36,250 --> 00:07:39,210
In any case, I think I'll go take an oatmeal bath.

150
00:07:40,460 --> 00:07:41,900
Eww.

151
00:07:41,900 --> 00:07:45,180
All right.

152
00:07:45,180 --> 00:07:45,360
We're back.

153
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:50,040
Why don't we take a look at Fluency Builder and start taking a look at all these phrases.

154
00:07:51,850 --> 00:07:52,630
Fluency Builder.

155
00:07:53,380 --> 00:07:59,440
Well, the first one is something that we often say when we're starting to feel ill.

156
00:08:00,060 --> 00:08:01,920
This phrase is coming down with.

157
00:08:02,120 --> 00:08:05,760
So I could say, I think I'm coming down with a cold, for example.

158
00:08:06,120 --> 00:08:09,200
So basically, you say, I'm coming down with something.

159
00:08:09,600 --> 00:08:12,220
So a cold or I think I'm coming down with the flu.

160
00:08:12,980 --> 00:08:15,320
So you're saying that I think I'm getting ill?

161
00:08:16,020 --> 00:08:16,300
Exactly.

162
00:08:16,780 --> 00:08:19,420
It means I'm just starting to feel sick.

163
00:08:19,420 --> 00:08:22,420
And so you could say, I've had a cold for a week.

164
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:24,890
You wouldn't use the phrase I'm coming down with in this situation.

165
00:08:25,220 --> 00:08:27,690
You'd only use it when you're starting to feel sick.

166
00:08:27,910 --> 00:08:28,450
Very good.

167
00:08:28,510 --> 00:08:31,650
So you could ask your boss, I think I'm coming down with the flu.

168
00:08:31,830 --> 00:08:32,630
Can I go home early?

169
00:08:33,970 --> 00:08:34,370
Right.

170
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:36,710
And I think this is a very useful phrase.

171
00:08:37,090 --> 00:08:39,250
It's very natural to say.

172
00:08:39,610 --> 00:08:41,930
So why don't we listen to a couple of other examples?

173
00:08:43,460 --> 00:08:44,040
Example one.

174
00:08:45,610 --> 00:08:46,990
Honey will you take my temperature?

175
00:08:47,780 --> 00:08:51,500
I think I'm coming down with something.

176
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:53,580
Example two.

177
00:08:53,580 --> 00:08:55,030
You must be coming down with a cold.

178
00:08:57,700 --> 00:09:01,380
Example three.

179
00:09:01,380 --> 00:09:02,520
Tommy came down with the flu.

180
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:05,250
All right.

181
00:09:06,100 --> 00:09:10,460
So coming down with, coming down with the flu, coming down with a cough, etc.

182
00:09:10,460 --> 00:09:10,590
All right.

183
00:09:11,460 --> 00:09:12,460
What's our next phrase?

184
00:09:12,460 --> 00:09:13,030
Well, this one's great.

185
00:09:13,460 --> 00:09:14,690
It's a filler phrase.

186
00:09:14,990 --> 00:09:16,830
So in the meantime.

187
00:09:17,470 --> 00:09:18,310
In the meantime.

188
00:09:19,190 --> 00:09:22,090
So I could say, all right, I'm going to go cook dinner.

189
00:09:23,030 --> 00:09:26,190
In the meantime, Marco, can you please clear the table?

190
00:09:26,620 --> 00:09:27,620
Okay.

191
00:09:27,620 --> 00:09:31,810
So while you are cooking dinner, I am doing something else.

192
00:09:31,990 --> 00:09:32,690
I'm cleaning the table.

193
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:34,540
So two things happening at the same time.

194
00:09:34,540 --> 00:09:35,090
In the meantime.

195
00:09:35,890 --> 00:09:36,210
Very good.

196
00:09:36,430 --> 00:09:37,570
So it's a filler, you say.

197
00:09:37,770 --> 00:09:39,530
Because usually it follows another phrase.

198
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:42,180
So I'm going to be doing this report for a while.

199
00:09:42,180 --> 00:09:46,300
In the meantime, can you just wait here on the sofa, please?

200
00:09:46,300 --> 00:09:46,490
Okay.

201
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:48,300
Very good.

202
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:48,690
So in the meantime.

203
00:09:49,300 --> 00:09:51,380
So you're connecting two different actions, two different ideas.

204
00:09:51,380 --> 00:09:52,380
Exactly.

205
00:09:52,380 --> 00:09:53,380
Very good.

206
00:09:53,380 --> 00:09:54,380
In the meantime.

207
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:55,490
And what about our last phrase?

208
00:09:56,390 --> 00:09:57,010
All right.

209
00:09:57,500 --> 00:09:59,070
There are some people who really like to do this.

210
00:10:00,060 --> 00:10:01,430
And I might be one of them.

211
00:10:01,780 --> 00:10:05,180
It's called to blow things out of proportion.

212
00:10:05,180 --> 00:10:06,180
All right.

213
00:10:06,350 --> 00:10:09,230
And you're going to hear this phrase a lot with native English speakers.

214
00:10:09,820 --> 00:10:22,100
And so to blow things out of proportion, well, it pretty much means that you're exaggerating or you're making a really big deal out of something that's not very serious.

215
00:10:22,340 --> 00:10:28,420
So for example, if you come over to my house and you break a glass and I start freaking out and yelling at you because you broke my glass.

216
00:10:28,740 --> 00:10:30,780
I say, Marco, you're blowing things out of proportion.

217
00:10:30,860 --> 00:10:32,140
I'll just buy you a new one.

218
00:10:32,240 --> 00:10:33,020
It's not that serious.

219
00:10:33,340 --> 00:10:34,340
All right.

220
00:10:34,340 --> 00:10:34,680
So don't exaggerate.

221
00:10:35,340 --> 00:10:36,340
Right.

222
00:10:36,340 --> 00:10:37,340
Right.

223
00:10:37,340 --> 00:10:44,340
And you can say like this with siblings, spouses or bosses who react very seriously.

224
00:10:44,340 --> 00:10:46,540
So you can say, hey, stop.

225
00:10:46,540 --> 00:10:48,930
Stop yelling like you're blowing things out of proportion.

226
00:10:49,100 --> 00:10:51,620
This is not a really big deal.

227
00:10:51,620 --> 00:10:51,750
Exactly.

228
00:10:52,620 --> 00:10:53,620
All right.

229
00:10:53,620 --> 00:10:54,870
So blowing things out of proportion.

230
00:10:55,340 --> 00:10:55,990
Let's not do that.

231
00:10:56,340 --> 00:10:59,710
Let's listen to our dialogue for the last time and then we'll be back.

232
00:11:07,670 --> 00:11:08,810
What's wrong with you?

233
00:11:09,230 --> 00:11:10,490
Why are you scratching so much?

234
00:11:11,340 --> 00:11:12,540
I feel itchy.

235
00:11:13,100 --> 00:11:14,240
I can't stand it anymore.

236
00:11:14,840 --> 00:11:16,420
I think I may be coming down with something.

237
00:11:16,840 --> 00:11:18,100
I feel lightheaded and weak.

238
00:11:18,960 --> 00:11:19,740
Let me have a look.

239
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:22,530
Whoa, get away from me.

240
00:11:22,810 --> 00:11:23,490
What's wrong?

241
00:11:24,170 --> 00:11:25,870
I think you have chicken pox.

242
00:11:26,650 --> 00:11:27,090
You're contagious.

243
00:11:28,270 --> 00:11:28,470
Get away.

244
00:11:28,830 --> 00:11:29,470
Don't breathe on me.

245
00:11:29,890 --> 00:11:31,710
Maybe it's just a rash or an allergy.

246
00:11:32,370 --> 00:11:34,170
We can't be sure until I see a doctor.

247
00:11:34,610 --> 00:11:36,810
Well, in the meantime, you're a biohazard.

248
00:11:37,350 --> 00:11:41,870
I didn't get it when I was a kid and I've heard that you can even die if you get it as an adult.

249
00:11:42,390 --> 00:11:42,930
Are you serious?

250
00:11:43,470 --> 00:11:44,850
You always blow things out of proportion.

251
00:11:45,550 --> 00:11:47,970
In any case, I think I'll go take an oatmeal bath.

252
00:11:50,740 --> 00:11:52,740
Eww.

253
00:11:55,660 --> 00:11:56,200
All right.

254
00:11:56,460 --> 00:11:58,720
So Catherine, have you ever had the chicken pox?

255
00:11:58,940 --> 00:11:59,300
I did.

256
00:11:59,940 --> 00:12:10,620
I have very, very clear memories of when I was a child and I had the chicken pox because I believe it was Thanksgiving and I was about four or five years old.

